{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-87687eab5855a6db","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018: GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project GPU-hour report computation and export API | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Administrator chargeback reporting views in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Chargeback reporting documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n19\t\n20\t## Dependency DAG\n21\t\n22\t```mermaid\n23\tgraph TD\n24\t    E001[\"E001 Report computation and export API\"] --> E002[\"E002 Dashboard admin reporting views\"]\n25\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Chargeback reporting documentation\"]\n26\t    E002 --> E003\n27\t```\n28\t\n29\t## DAG Justification\n30\t\n31\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n32\t|------|------|-----------|\n33\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The Dashboard views build against the report API and computed per-project GPU-hour data produced by the report computation epic; a code-builds-against-artifact dependency, so serial. |\n34\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; documentation describes what was built. |\n35\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same rule; documentation covers the admin views as well as the report semantics and export integration. |\n36\t| (no edge) RHAISTRAT-9990 → E001 | Rule 21 (third party resolves) | The metering data dependency is resolved by a separate in-flight strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990), which owns the collection pipeline and its schema. It is modeled as a precondition with an acceptance-criteria gate on E001 (validated against the delivered metering data; schema changes absorbed in the input mapping), not as an epic in this decomposition — the strategy explicitly puts the metering pipeline out of scope and forbids re-implementing collection. |\n37\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 start | — | Not parallel: E002 consumes E001's API artifact (Rule 13 edge above). No other parallelism exists in a three-epic chain. |\n38\t\n39\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n40\t\n41\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n42\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n43\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002 | Split along the architectural seam: E001 computes per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges from the metering data; E002 surfaces them in the administrator-facing Dashboard view. Both are needed for the HLR; E001 is P0 because it blocks the P0 user-facing capability. |\n44\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 computation work: CSV export and the stable finance API are output modes of the same per-project GPU-hour aggregation over the same metering data, served from the same report-computation surface. HLR-2 is not independently deferrable — splitting it would build the same aggregation and service twice and add an artificial serialization edge between two epics sharing one API surface. |\n45\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E002 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 Dashboard work: the current-month consumption-vs-Kueue-quota view is another presentation in the same Dashboard administrator view surface consuming the same report data (quota read from the Kueue CRs the Dashboard already accesses). Splitting would duplicate the admin view surface; the HLR shares data structures and UI surface with HLR-1, so it is not independently deferrable. |\n46\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E003 | Documentation of report semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour) and export integration; substantive content authoring is a distinct work stream from code, so a separate docs-authoring epic (priority from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception). |\n47\t\n48\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n49\t\n50\t## Health Warnings\n51\t\n52\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Affected Components change/owner detail (bare bullet list), no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no Dependencies table, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Owner teams were inferred from the architecture context files (`dashboard.md`: RHOAI Dashboard; `distributed-workloads.md`: Distributed Workloads); epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n53\t- **External timing risk (tracked, not blocking decomposition)**: the metering data schema is owned by RHAISTRAT-9990 and finalizes only when that strategy lands. This does not change which epics exist (all three deliver the reporting capability either way), so it is not an Investigation epic — it is carried as an acceptance criterion on E001 (validated against delivered metering data; schema changes absorbed in the input mapping) and as a schedule risk note. If RHAISTRAT-9990's final schema diverges materially from what is delivered mid-flight, E001's input mapping is the only intended rework surface.\n54\t- **Component mapping inference (non-obvious)**: the strategy lists Dashboard and Distributed Workloads without describing what changes in each. The stable finance-facing report API and the report computation are assigned to Distributed Workloads — the component owning the workload usage/quota domain (Kueue metrics exposed to Prometheus per `distributed-workloads.md`) — because the Dashboard architecture context (`dashboard.md`) states it owns no data plane and exposes no private component APIs (Kubernetes API only), which rules out hosting a finance-tooling API there. The Dashboard epic covers the administrator-facing views, reading Kueue quota via the CRs it already consumes.\n55\t- **Docs epic team unspecified**: the strategy names no owning team for documentation. E003 is assigned to the Distributed Workloads team, which owns the report semantics and export API that dominate the documented content; confirm at planning.\n56\t- **Priority inversion check**: none found. HLR priorities (P0 reporting, P1 export/quota view, P2 docs) are consistent with the technical sequencing — the computation foundation is the largest risk item and gates everything else.\n57\t\n58\t## Tiered Delivery\n59\t\n60\tEvaluated per the external-dependency rule (RHAISTRAT-9990 in flight, uncertain finalization timing): **no tier split**. Every user-facing HLR (1–3) consumes per-project GPU-hour metered data, so no deliverable user value is independent of the dependency. The in-flight status means metering data is already being delivered; the residual exposure is schema-finalization rework, which is confined by an acceptance criterion on E001 (input-mapping isolation) rather than by a separate dependency-gated epic. Dependencies are linear: E001 → E002 → E003.\n61\t\n62\t## Component Mapping Notes\n63\t\n64\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Epic(s) | Mapping |\n65\t|--------------------|----------------------|---------|---------|\n66\t| Dashboard | Dashboard | E002 | Direct match; canonical list name identical. |\n67\t| Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | E001, E003 | Direct match for E001 (report computation/export API, inferred owner of the backend surface — see Health Warnings). E003 uses the canonical `Documentation` component for the docs-authoring work stream. |\n68\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b473b6dbc832cfdb","agent_id":"a4050c611fcde7715","session_id":"9043ac75-7a00-4a8d-a271-1ae0d81512e8","uuid":"95a114f6-2001-4b8c-ba9b-89f2f89fa321","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:24:09.833Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-87687eab5855a6db","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source"